\details{
The point of grouping is that whole projects, not individual studies, are
held out. Studies from one project share a design, a discipline and a base
rate, so a random split would put siblings on both sides of the fold and
answer a different question: how the model does on a new \emph{study} drawn from
the same pool, rather than on a new \emph{project}.

\strong{On summarizing across folds.} The estimates are deliberately returned
per group and are not averaged by \code{print()}. Describing their spread is
fine; turning it into a standard error or a confidence interval is not, for
two reasons.

The first needs no theory: the folds are of very different sizes. On the
\code{altmejd} projects they hold 10, 13, 18, 21 and 90 studies, so an
unweighted summary treats five estimates of wildly different precision as if
they were exchangeable draws.

The second is that the folds are not independent. They share most of their
training data -- with five projects, any two training sets overlap in three of
four -- so the usual standard error of the mean is badly down-biased, and no
generally unbiased estimator of the variance of cross-validation exists
(Bengio & Grandvalet, 2004).

Read the spread; do not reduce it to an interval. The mean is still available
from \code{summary()} for ranking grid rows, which is a different use.

\strong{What nesting estimates.} A plain run is honest about \emph{one}
setting fixed in advance. Score a whole grid and keep the best average and it
is not, because the same folds chose the winner and then graded it. Setting
\code{nested = TRUE} separates the two: the held-out project takes no part in
choosing hyperparameters.

The number it returns is easy to misread. \strong{It is not a performance
estimate for any particular setting.} It estimates the \emph{procedure} -- "run the
grid search, then fit the winner". Every model built inside the nesting is
discarded; the model you ship is still a single setting fit on all the data,
and the nested estimate says what to expect from it rather than what it
scores. Expect the outer folds to select different settings, and expect the
setting that won on the full grid to be chosen by none of them: with few
groups there is no stable optimum to find, and seeing that directly is more
informative than the average that hides it. The nested estimate is also
\emph{conservative}, because each nested model trains on one group fewer than
the model you ship.

The gap between the naive and nested numbers grows with how much there was to
choose from. It is an expectation, not a guarantee: on a grid of only a few
settings there is little to overfit, and the nested estimate can land slightly
above the naive one by chance. Do not read a small or negative gap as evidence
that selection is harmless -- read it as a grid too small for selection to
matter.

\strong{Models.} \code{"adaboost"} calls
\code{\link[adatutor]{adaboost}} and
\code{\link[=predict.adaboost]{predict()}}, and scores the raw margin.
\code{"stump"} and \code{"tree"} are \code{rpart} models
differing only in depth. \code{"logit"} is a binomial \code{glm}. \code{"rf"}
needs the suggested \code{randomForest} package. All of them are scored on a
continuous quantity, never on hard class labels: a two-valued score collapses
the ROC curve to a single interior point, at which \code{auroc} silently
becomes balanced accuracy.
